/**
	This class provides a convenient way to communicate with all of the text-to-speech voices a user has installed on their system.
	tts_voice(const string&in engines = "");
	## Arguments:
		* const string&in engines = "": a comma separated list of the speech engines this voice may use, such as "sapi5" on Windows, "avspeech" on Apple platforms, "speechd" on Linux or an engine's package name on Android, along with "fallback" for the builtin synthesizer. A blank string, the default, uses every engine.
	## Remarks:
		NVGT implements a tiny [builtin fallback speech synthesizer](https://github.com/mattiasgustavsson/libs/blob/main/speech.h) that is used either when there is no speech engine available on the system, or if it is explicitly set with the tts_voice::set_voice() function. It sounds terrible, and is only intended for emergencies, after all understandable speech is better than no speech at all. It was used heavily when porting NVGT to other platforms, because it allowed focus to be primarily fixed on getting nvgt to run while individual tts engine support on each platform could be added later. If you don't want your end users to be able to select this speech synthesizer, pass this constructor a list of engines that leaves "fallback" out; the default blank string includes it.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	tts_voice v;
	v.speak_wait("Type some text and I'll repeat it. Leave the field blank to stop");
	string text = "";
	while (true) {
		text = input_box("Text", "Type some text to have the TTS speak.");
		if (text.empty()) break;
		v.speak(text);
	}
}
